Check the data of a state blob in the copy of the host reader, and remove the sequence of a failed load
Fold-into: fuzz-core/0025-reject-a-nan-or-an-inf-in-the-data-of-a-state-blob.patch

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- The check of patch 0025 adds a second read of the blob and a misaligned load.

  0025 calls ggml_validate_row_data on the bytes of the blob in read_tensor. The bytes of a blob have
  no alignment, thus the F32 and F16 loads of that function are misaligned (UBSan reports them in
  the core suite). And its per-element tests in ggml-base run at 3.6 GB/s for F16 on x86, against
  25 GB/s for memcpy. On the box, one thread, the load of a 2B state of 4096 tokens (F16 KV,
  67 MiB) takes 21.5 ms against 4.2 ms without the check, and a 4B state (178 MiB) 55.6 ms against
  11.3 ms.

  The change: the check of the types of the caches of the app (F32, F16, BF16, Q8_0) is one
  integer pass that the compiler vectorizes: the largest magnitude bits of the values (for Q8_0, of
  the f16 block scales) against the bits of Inf. It reads each value with memcpy. A different type
  goes through an aligned copy to ggml_validate_row_data. The host reader saves its reads and
  writes them later, as upstream does. Its new flush() writes each saved read in parts of 256 KiB:
  it checks a part and then writes it while the part is in the cache, thus the load reads the blob
  from memory one time. llama_context::state_set_data and state_seq_set_data call flush() in their
  try block. A part with a NaN or an Inf stops the flush, and the load fails. The file reader
  checks the bytes that it reads before it writes them, as in 0025. The device reader has no check.
  With the change the two loads above take 5.2 ms and 13.0 ms, and a blob with finite values gives
  the same memory as before.

- A failed sequence load keeps a part of the sequence.

  The KV cache and the recurrent memory remove the cells of the sequence when their own read
  fails. But the hybrid memory reads the KV cache first: when the read of the recurrent memory
  fails, the KV cache keeps the cells that it loaded. A failure in the flush() above also comes
  after the two reads. Thus llama_memory_seq_pos_min shows a part of the sequence after
  llama_state_seq_set_data returns 0.

  The change: when a sequence load fails after the read of the memory starts, state_seq_set_data
  removes the sequence (llama_memory_seq_rm with -1, -1). A load that fails at its magic number
  does not change the memory, as before. When the flush of a full state load fails,
  state_set_data clears the memory with its data, as the KV cache does when its read fails.

  The reproducers: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-fail-kv-part (a damaged recurrent
  part), and property P6 of fuzz_recurrent with fuzz_recurrent--state-nan-kv-window (a NaN that the
  flush finds after it wrote the KV rows).

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

